# Bundled voice data is read byte-for-byte by the speech engine.
#
# The pinyin lexicon, the token tables and the espeak-ng data are text files, but
# their readers were not written for Windows line endings: the lexicon is parsed as
# `character pinyin tone _` per line, and a trailing carriage return turns the last
# field into `_\r`, which the engine does not recognise. The models and the rule FSTs
# are binary outright. Marking the tree binary keeps the bytes that were committed as
# the bytes that are checked out, whatever `core.autocrlf` is set to on the machine
# doing the cloning — a voice that reads differently on Windows than on Linux is not
# something anyone can reason about.
app/src/main/assets/tts/** binary

# SQLite, read as bytes.
app/src/main/assets/dict.db binary

# The parser fixtures are real books, compressed and encrypted; git must not look
# inside them at all.
*.mobi binary
*.epub binary
*.azw3 binary
